;; CLASSIFICATION (`:kotoba.security/classification`, root ADR-2607280100 D5).
;;
;; Every entry declares one, and the declaration is part of the LANGUAGE
;; SPEC rather than something a caller passes in. That is the whole point:
;; ADR-2607280100's problem 3 is that a classification supplied by the
;; caller can be OMITTED, and an omission is indistinguishable from a
;; correct declaration -- it fails open and nothing says so. A label that
;; lives on the effect descriptor cannot be omitted by a caller, and a
;; capability added here WITHOUT one cannot be compiled at all
;; (`kotoba.compiler.effect-classification` refuses; see amu).
;;
;; The label is the CLEARANCE A SUBJECT MUST HOLD to be granted this
;; authority -- `kotoba.security.abac`'s no-read-up reading of a resource
;; classification -- and NOT the classification of any one call's payload.
;; A payload label is a runtime fact; this is a static property of the
;; authority. `secret/get` cannot be exercised below `:restricted` no
;; matter what secret is fetched; `hash/sha256` crosses no boundary at all.
;;
;; The four labels are `kotoba.security.information-flow/ranks` and nothing
;; else. There is exactly one lattice (ADR-2607280100 D1) and this file
;; does not restate it -- a label this catalog writes that that lattice
;; cannot rank is refused at compile time, in the same breath as a missing
;; one, because an unrankable declaration and no declaration leave the
;; judgment with equally nothing to compare.
;;
;;   :public       crosses no trust boundary and carries no subject data
;;   :internal     this program's own surfaces -- its log, its screen, its
;;                 stdout/stderr, how it was invoked, where it was started
;;   :confidential someone else's data or an external service: durable
;;                 state, storage, objects, the network, a filesystem, a
;;                 model provider
;;   :restricted   authority over credentials, other processes, other
;;                 applications' screens, raw buses, or code selection
