  ;; io/write — the bytes a COMMAND writes to its standard output. Wire id
  ;; 37.
  ;;
  ;; Request is the bytes; the result is the DECIMAL COUNT of bytes written,
  ;; as text. Two constraints pick that shape and neither is a preference:
  ;;
  ;;   * `[:string :string]` is one of the four generic type pairs
  ;;     `kotoba.kir`'s native gate admits for `typed-cap-call` (the others
  ;;     are i64, option-i64, result-i64). `:string -> :i64` is not among
  ;;     them and would need a named provider contract, which is a change to
  ;;     the gate rather than to this catalog.
  ;;   * the result must be SHORT. A native guest's strings live in one
  ;;     65,536-byte arena that is a bump allocator and never reclaims
  ;;     (`kexe_loader.c`, no assignment to `string_pool_used` other than
  ;;     `+=`), so echoing the payload back -- the shape `fs/app-data`'s
  ;;     write form uses -- would charge every write twice. A count costs a
  ;;     handful of bytes whatever the payload is, and is still verifiable.
  ;;
  ;; The effect is `:host/io-write`, which is deliberately NOT the
  ;; `:host/log-append` family: a command's standard output is its answer,
  ;; not an audit record, and the two must not share a grant.
  :io/write
  {:source-operation io/write :effect :host/io-write
   :compiler-wire-id 37
   :kotoba.security/classification :internal}
  ;; cli/args — the arguments a COMMAND was invoked with. Wire id 38.
  ;;
  ;; `:string -> :string`, so the index travels as decimal text: the empty
  ;; request answers the COUNT, a decimal answers that argument zero-based,
  ;; and past the end answers the empty string. The empty request is what
  ;; makes the count unambiguous -- an index is a non-empty decimal, so no
  ;; index can collide with it, and a guest cannot instead probe for the
  ;; first empty answer because an empty ARGUMENT is legal and looks the
  ;; same.
  ;;
  ;; Like :io/write this carries no resource scope, and for the same reason:
  ;; the guest names nothing and chooses nothing. The grant means exactly
  ;; "may see how this process was invoked".
  :cli/args
  {:source-operation cli/args :effect :host/cli-args
   :compiler-wire-id 38
   :kotoba.security/classification :internal}
  ;; io/write-error — the bytes a COMMAND writes to its DIAGNOSTIC output.
  ;; Wire id 39. Same `:string -> :string` shape as :io/write and the same
  ;; decimal byte count for a result; the difference is the destination and,
  ;; more importantly, the AUTHORITY.
  ;;
  ;; Its own effect and deliberately not :host/io-write. A command's standard
  ;; output is its answer and its diagnostic output is not: a pipeline reads
  ;; one and a person reads the other, and a grant that carried both would let
  ;; a guest put into the answer what it was only permitted to complain with.
  ;; Six commands in this family had shipped saying "the error paths are not
  ;; matched" because there was no capability to match them with.
  :io/write-error
  {:source-operation io/write-error :effect :host/io-write-error
   :compiler-wire-id 39
   :kotoba.security/classification :internal}
  ;; sys/cwd — the directory the process was started in. Wire id 40.
  ;;
  ;; The empty request answers an absolute path; there is no other form. It
  ;; carries no resource scope, like :io/write and :cli/args, and for the
  ;; same reason: the guest names nothing and chooses nothing. The grant
  ;; means exactly "may see where this process was started".
  ;;
  ;; Its own effect, and specifically NOT :host/env-read, though `pwd` is
  ;; often described as reading $PWD. Measured 2026-09-10: /bin/pwd -L
  ;; VALIDATES $PWD against the real directory and prints the physical path
  ;; when they disagree -- with PWD=/tmp it printed the physical path anyway.
  ;; So the environment variable is not the answer, it is a hint the real
  ;; answer is checked against, and a grant to read environment variables
  ;; therefore cannot supply it. That is why this is a wire and not another
  ;; request form.
  :sys/cwd
  {:source-operation sys/cwd :effect :host/sys-cwd
   :compiler-wire-id 40
   :kotoba.security/classification :internal}}
